    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "The doctor is not vicariously liable for the actions of the blood bank employee who made the error.",
      "evidence": "The blood bank employee is not an employee of the doctor; they are part of the hospital's blood bank department. The doctor is an independent contractor or staff physician, but does not employ the blood bank staff. Even if the doctor were an employee of the hospital, the hospital would be liable for its own employees, not the doctor. The question asks about liability of the *doctor*.",
      "weight": 0.95,
      "depends_on": [3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "The doctor's violation of the hospital's written rule (oral vs. written orders) is not the proximate cause of the injury.",
      "evidence": "The rule requires written orders. The doctor gave an oral order. However, the nurse correctly wrote down the order and transmitted it correctly. The error occurred downstream in the blood bank. The violation of the rule did not lead to the specific error (wrong blood type sent); the error would have likely occurred regardless of whether the order was written or oral, as the blood bank staff made the mistake in filling the order.",
      "weight": 0.85,
      "depends_on": [1, 3]
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "claim": "The fact that many other doctors acted similarly does not establish a standard of care that excuses the violation, nor does it prove causation.",
      "evidence": "Custom (many doctors doing it) is evidence of standard of care but does not override the lack of causation. More importantly, it doesn't make the doctor liable for the blood bank's error.",
      "weight": 0.5,
      "depends_on": [5]
    },
    {
      "id": 7,
      "claim": "The mother's claim against the doctor will not prevail because the doctor's actions (or inactions regarding the rule) were not the proximate cause of the death, which was caused by the blood bank employee's error.",
      "evidence": "Proximate cause requires that the defendant's negligence be a substantial factor in bringing about the harm. Here, the intervening act of the blood bank employee (sending wrong blood) is the direct cause. The doctor's oral order was correctly received and recorded, so the oral nature of the order did not contribute to the error.",
